# Discussion Phase
{{#workflow}}
Source Guide URI: `guide://_discuss`.
{{/workflow}}

## Directives

- When the user does not name an issue, preserve the current issue, description, plan, tracking reference, and queue. Seek alignment before changing project state.
{{#workflow}}
- When the user names an issue, treat it as a new or resumed item. Ensure `{{workflow-file}}` records its issue, description, plan, tracking reference, and queue position.
- Copy an associated issue-tracker reference into the tracking field, and update the queue to reflect the item's current priority.
{{#openspec}}
- Copy an associated OpenSpec reference into the tracking field.
{{/openspec}}
- Update `{{workflow-file}}` to `phase: discussion` when changing phase.
- After editing `{{workflow-file}}`, send its complete content using `send_file_content`.
{{/workflow}}
- Keep handoff context `{{path.documents}}/context.json` updated at meaningful milestones when present.

## Guardrails

- Discussion is non-implementation work.
- Allowed edits: {{#workflow}}`{{workflow-file}}`, {{/workflow}}{{#openspec}}`openspec/`, {{/openspec}}`{{path.documents}}`.
- Do not modify production code or tests in this phase.
- If returning to discussion after finishing work, confirm whether the current issue should be marked
  complete before clearing it or advancing the queue.
{{#openspec}}
## OpenSpec Association

If OpenSpec is active for the current project, discussion can identify or refine the relevant
change before implementation. Use `openspec-new-change` when a new OpenSpec change needs to be
started, `openspec-propose` when the user wants a full proposal/spec/task set quickly.

In addition, `openspec-explore` may be used when requirements still need investigation.
{{/openspec}}
